 Hmmm, interesting idea.
 The possibilities in such a system would be interesting and I also
would like to hear how mythical such a game would be. You would have
to trade off alot of possibilities though.
For instance,
  Does everyone have a pet or only someone? Are they allowed to
have different pets if this is the case? If everyone does, will the pets
fight among themselves since cat and dog are meant to be natural enemies?
 What about levels? I imagine everyone would have to be on the same
level before they can go down (this would make the system abit easier
to program) although the other possibility (of everyone being allowed
to be at different level) is not obviously not out of contention.
 What would you do to win the game? Would the overall objective be to
just collect the amulet from the wizard (and thus be a group effort)
or does everyone have to combat some different form of threat to win the game
(and so everyone can be assessed on individual performances :- in this
case some can be winner(s) of the game, others losers).
 What would happened when everyone stepped on a teleportation trap one after
the other.? If it goes to the same location, do they appear near to each other
or would they appear inside each other (that's how I would expect a 
teleportation trap to actually work).
 Since players can work as teams, why not monsters of the dungeon. 
In all fairness this should probably also be included. For instance,
dwarfs combine with goblins to form parties and actually work as parties
instead of just individuals as they seem to do in normal hack.
 Some other interesting points would be that when a character within a
team dies, his ghost appears and the team would now have to fight him
now instead of having to wait to next game to do so. Magic spells
would now have to be considered very carefully, since does a spell
affect the whole team (i.e. does a Map spell allow the whole team
see the game grid) or just an individual (i.e. When the character throws
a lightning bolt, can it be allowed to hit another player. If such is
the case, death would be far easier in this game then normal Hack). Does
the strength of magic spells increase when two similar spells are cast,
what happens with spells the counteract each other (such as cursed spells).

>        It would be great!

 I agree. I also think it would be fun to explore some of the possibilities
to that such a game could provide/would have to decide even if they
are only wish-lists/hypothesis. Thanks for the discussion.

